WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.000
In this video, I'm going to synthesize some of the main ideas of this week's videos.

2
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.960
In other words, we'll chunk our week on chunking.

3
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:16.559
Here we go.

4
00:00:16.559 --> 00:00:21.280
Chunks are pieces of information, neuroscientifically speaking, that are bound together through

5
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.040
use and often through meaning.

6
00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:30.000
You can think of a chunk as a scintillating network of neurons that compactly synthesizes

7
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.799
key ideas or actions.

8
00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:39.360
Chunks can get bigger and more complex, but at the same time, they're a single, easy-to-access

9
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.799
item that you can fit like a ribbon into the slot on your working memory.

10
00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:52.119
Chunks are best built with focused, undivided attention, understanding of the basic idea,

11
00:00:52.119 --> 00:00:58.720
and practice to help deepen your patterns and to help you gain big-picture context.

12
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.919
Simple recall—trying to remember the key points without looking at the page—is one

13
00:01:03.919 --> 00:01:07.500
of the best ways to help the chunking process along.

14
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.040
It seems to help build neural hooks that help you better understand the material.

15
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.160
Also try recalling material in places that are different from where you originally learned

16
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:24.879
so it becomes more deeply ingrained and accessible, regardless of what room you're in.

17
00:01:24.879 --> 00:01:28.120
This can be very helpful for tests.

18
00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:33.239
Transfer is the idea that a chunk you've mastered in one area can often help you much

19
00:01:33.239 --> 00:01:40.519
more easily learn chunks of information in different areas that can share surprising commonalities.

20
00:01:40.519 --> 00:01:46.080
Interleave your learning by practicing your choice of different concepts, approaches,

21
00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:49.760
and techniques all in one session.

22
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.199
Chunks are very important, but they don't necessarily build flexibility, which is also

23
00:01:55.199 --> 00:01:59.959
important in becoming an expert with the material you're learning.

24
00:01:59.959 --> 00:02:05.279
Illusions of competence in learning—learn to recognize when you're fooling yourself

25
00:02:05.279 --> 00:02:08.720
about whether you're actually learning the material.

26
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.720
Test yourself frequently, using little mini-tests to see whether you're actually learning

27
00:02:13.759 --> 00:02:18.240
the material or whether you've been fooling yourself, thinking you're learning when

28
00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.600
you're actually not.

29
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:24.240
Recall is actually a form of mini-testing.

30
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.240
Try to avoid depending too much on highlighting, which can fool you into thinking that the

31
00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:33.199
material is going into your brain when it actually isn't.

32
00:02:33.199 --> 00:02:38.559
Mistakes are a good thing to make when you're learning—they allow you to catch illusions

33
00:02:38.559 --> 00:02:40.720
of competence.

34
00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.119
Avoid practicing only the easy stuff, which can bring the illusion that you've mastered

35
00:02:45.119 --> 00:02:46.880
the material.

36
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:53.360
Deliberately practice what you find more difficult to gain full mastery of the material.

37
00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.399
Einstellung is when your initial thought—an idea you've already had in mind or a neural

38
00:02:59.399 --> 00:03:06.240
pattern you've already developed well and strengthened—prevents a better idea or solution

39
00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:11.880
from being found, or keeps you from being flexible enough to accept new, better, or

40
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.600
more appropriate solutions.

41
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.639
The law of serendipity is helpful.

42
00:03:17.639 --> 00:03:20.960
Lady luck favors the one who tries.

43
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.240
Just pick one tiny thing out to learn, then another.

44
00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:29.759
Just keep trying, and you'll be pleasantly surprised at the results.

45
00:03:29.759 --> 00:03:31.399
I'm Barbara Oakley.

46
00:03:31.399 --> 00:03:33.479
Thanks for learning about learning.